{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~3–10 EUR), especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when recent history rolls over, the model declines too slowly and predicts rebounds that do not materialize. Increase negative drift proportional to recent 10–20d slope (i.e., amplify recent bearish momentum).",
    "False mean reversion: the model repeatedly projects recovery toward mid/high-70s even as the market continues to sell off into mid/low-60s. Reduce mean-reversion strength when trailing momentum is negative (trend filter).",
    "Horizon error grows with time: bias and over-optimism widen at longer horizons (weeks 3–4). Apply a horizon-dependent downward adjustment that increases with lead time.",
    "Overshoot after local upticks: after small bounces in history, the model extrapolates too much upside; realized series resumes falling. Cap upside response to short-term upticks when broader trend is down."
  ]
}
